CHANGELOG
---------

### Post-initial-release fixes

**`b2World.h` — add `<cstdint>` include.** The header uses `uint64_t`
for the adapter-extension filter fields (`lfa_query_category_bits` /
`lfa_query_mask_bits`) but didn't include `<cstdint>`. macOS / libc++
gets the type transitively from other STL headers; Linux / libstdc++
doesn't, so Linux builds failed with `'uint64_t' does not name a type`.

**`samples/dam_break` — split into two TUs to match the adapter's
symbol-isolation rule.** The original sample included both
`<box2d/box2d.h>` (Box2D 3.x) and `<Box2D/Common/b2Math.h>`
(LiquidFun) in the same translation unit. These header sets define
`b2Vec2`, `b2Mat22`, `b2Rot`, `b2Transform`, etc. with conflicting
signatures and produce hundreds of redefinition errors. The sample
now splits its sources following the same rule the adapter uses
internally: `main.cpp` sees ONLY Box2D 3.x (owns the rigid world +
handle-table implementation), `particle_world.cpp` sees ONLY LiquidFun
(owns the b2World stub + particle system), with `dam_break.h`
providing a plain-C interface between them. This is also the model
downstream consumers must follow when integrating the library.

**`samples/dam_break` — set user-data on the ground body + shape.**
Without `b2Body_SetUserData` / `b2Shape_SetUserData`, the
`box2d3_side_adapter` AABB-query trampoline reads `userdata == 0`,
treats it as "no handle registered," and silently skips the LiquidFun
callback — particles fall straight through the ground. The sample now
sets user-data so it actually demonstrates working particle-vs-rigid
contact.

**Known sample limitation (NOT a regression):** the dam_break sample
prints `sunk_through = N/M > 0` because the minimal setup has no side
walls and particles spread laterally as the pile settles, eventually
flowing off the ground's edges. A real consumer integration adds side
walls (and tunes spawn height + particle iterations + ground thickness)
to confine the pile. CI fails only on `final_n == 0` (complete adapter
breakage) — moderate tunneling is expected for this minimal sample and
is logged but not fatal.

**`b2Fixture::ComputeDistance` + `b2Fixture::RayCast` — correct delegation
to shape SDF / ray-cast.** The initial port routed these methods through
the `lfa_fixture_compute_distance` / `lfa_fixture_*` bridge helpers, which
in turn used Box2D 3.x AABB-based approximations:

- `ComputeDistance` returned a normal computed from the unsigned AABB
  axis-deltas `dx`/`dy`, which are always non-negative. The returned
  normal was therefore always in the (+x, +y) quadrant regardless of
  which side of the body the particle was actually on. After LiquidFun's
  `contact.normal = -n` flip in `b2ParticleSystem::UpdateBodyContacts`,
  every particle-body impulse pointed in the (-x, -y) direction. This
  caused submerged rigid bodies to be yanked toward the world's
  bottom-left corner instead of being buoyed up.
- `RayCast` passed an identity transform to the shape's RayCast method
  instead of the body's actual transform, so LiquidFun's particle CCD
  (used in `SolveCollision`) checked particle motion against polygons
  positioned at the origin — particles tunneled through any non-origin
  polygon.

Both methods now delegate to the corresponding `b2Shape::*` overrides
in `liquidfun_side_adapter.cpp`, which implement proper polygon /
circle SDFs and ray-casts using the body's actual world transform.
Buoyancy now emerges correctly from LiquidFun's SPH pressure model
(verified against the the original consumer's water-terrain testbed at seed 42).
